ow with k <4 would increase the total by 7 - 2k \u22651). Similarly, each column has m \u22654 pluses, otherwise flipping it would increase the total. Therefore, a local maximum in total pluses must have all rows and columns with \u22654 pluses. Hence, such a configuration exists, and by flipping rows and columns greedily, we can reach it.\n\nYes! This seems promising. Because the total number of pluses is bounded (up to 49), and each flip that increases the total number of pluses is possible until we can't flip anymore. At that point, all rows and columns must have \u22654 pluses. Therefore, such a configuration exists.\n\nBut we need to formalize this argument.\n\nLet\u2019s consider the total number of pluses as our measure. Starting from any configuration, if there exists a row or column with fewer than 4 pluses, flipping it will increase the total number of pluses. Therefore, we can repeatedly flip such rows or columns until no more such flips are possible. Since the total number of pluses increases with each flip and is bounded by 49, the process must terminate. When it terminates, every row and column has at least 4 pluses. Therefore, the desired configuration is achievable.\n\nBut wait, does flipping a row with k <4 pluses necessarily increase the total? Yes. If a row has k pluses, flipping it gives 7 - k pluses. The difference is 7 - 2k. If k <4, then 7 -2k > 7 -8 = -1, but since k is integer, k \u22643, so 7 -2k \u22657 -6=1. So flipping such a row increases the total by at least 1. Similarly for columns.\n\nTherefore, as long as there exists a row or column with fewer than 4 pluses, we can flip it to increase the total pluses. Since the total pluses is a non-negative integer bounded by 49, this process must terminate after a finite number of steps. When it terminates, there are no more rows or columns with fewer than 4 pluses, meaning all rows and columns have at least 4 pluses. Hence, the configuration satisfies the required condition.\n\nBut wait, does 