**: Flip any column with \\( m < 4 \\) pluses. This also increases the total pluses.\n   - Repeat until no such rows or columns remain.\n\n5. **Termination**:\n   - Total pluses increases strictly with each flip.\n   - Since the total is bounded by 49, the process terminates.\n\n6. **Conclusion**:\n   - Upon termination, all rows and columns have \\( \\geq 4 \\) pluses.\n\n**Final Answer**: By iteratively flipping rows and columns with fewer than 4 pluses, the total pluses strictly increases until all rows and columns meet the requirement. Hence, such a configuration is achievable. $\\boxed{\\text{Such a configuration always exists.}}$", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Initial Setup**:\n   - Consider any initial configuration of pluses (`+`) and minuses (`\u2212`) in the $7 \\times 7$ grid.\n\n2. **Flip Rows or Columns**:\n   - If in any row or column the number of `\u2212` is more than the number of `+`, flip all the signs in that row or column. \n   - Flipping a sign inverts each `+` to `\u2212` and each `\u2212` to `+`.\n\n3. **Justification**:\n   - By flipping the signs in a row or a column where there are more `\u2212` than `+`, the number of `+` in that row or column increases:\n     - If a row/column has $m$ `\u2212` and $n$ `+` with $m > n$, after flipping, it will have $m$ `+` and $n$ `\u2212`.\n     - This means $m - n$ more `+` than `\u2212`.\n\n4. **Iterative Process**:\n   - Continue this process for any row or column where the number of `\u2212` is more than the number of `+`.\n   - Each flip increases the total number of `+` in the grid, because each flip converts all the signs in the row/column yielding more `+` overall.\n\n5. **Termination**:\n   - The grid can only have 49 cells, so the process cannot continue indefinitely.\n   - Eventually, the configuration will stabilize where no row or column has more `\u2212` than `+`.\n\n6. **Conclusion**:\n   - Thus, after a finite number of operations, the configuration will be such that every row and eve