sin(g(x)) ).\n\nNow, the next step is to find the range of cos(\u03b8) where \u03b8 \u2208 [-\u03c0/3, \u03c0/3]. Cosine is an even function, so it's symmetric around 0. On the interval [0, \u03c0/3], cosine decreases from 1 to cos(\u03c0/3) = 0.5. Therefore, the range of cos(\u03b8) when \u03b8 \u2208 [-\u03c0/3, \u03c0/3] is [0.5, 1]. Therefore, cos( (\u03c0/3) sin(g(x)) ) \u2208 [0.5, 1]. Then, multiplying this by 4, the function f(x) = 4 cos( ... ) will have a range of [4 * 0.5, 4 * 1] = [2, 4].\n\nWait, but hold on a second. Is this correct? Let me check. If \u03b8 is in [-\u03c0/3, \u03c0/3], then cos(\u03b8) is maximum at \u03b8=0 (cos(0)=1) and minimum at \u03b8= \u00b1\u03c0/3 (cos(\u03c0/3)=0.5). So yes, cos(\u03b8) ranges from 0.5 to 1 in that interval. Therefore, multiplying by 4 gives [2, 4]. Therefore, the range of f(x) is [2, 4]. Hmm.\n\nBut let me verify each step again to be thorough, because sometimes there can be subtleties.\n\nFirst, confirm the quadratic part: x\u00b2 + 6x +10 = (x + 3)^2 +1. Correct. Therefore, this is always \u22651. Then, subtract sin x. Since sin x \u2208 [-1,1], the minimum value of g(x) would be 1 -1 = 0, and as x moves away from -3, the quadratic term becomes large, so subtracting sin x (which is bounded) would just make g(x) go to infinity. Therefore, g(x) \u2208 [0, \u221e). Correct.\n\nThen, sin(g(x)): since g(x) can take any value from 0 to \u221e, sin(g(x)) will oscillate between -1 and 1. So sin(g(x)) \u2208 [-1, 1]. Correct. Therefore, multiplying by \u03c0/3 gives us \u03b8 = (\u03c0/3) sin(g(x)) \u2208 [-\u03c0/3, \u03c0/3]. Then, cos(\u03b8) \u2208 [0.5, 1], since cos(\u03c0/3)=0.5 and cos(0)=1. Therefore, multiplying by 4, f(x) \u2208 [2, 4]. So the range is [2,4].\n\nBut wait, wait another moment. Is there a possibility that sin(g(x)) cannot reach certain values in [-1,1]? For example, if g(x) is restricted in such a way that sin(g(x)) cannot attain -1 or 1? Let's think about that.\n\nSince g(x) \u2208 [0, \u2