4. \\(\\lfloor \\sqrt{n} \\rfloor = \\lfloor \\sqrt{n} \\rfloor\\)\n\nRight side:\n\n1. \\(\\sqrt{\\sqrt{n^2}} = \\sqrt{n}\\)\n2. \\(\\lfloor \\sqrt{n} \\rfloor\\)\n\nSo equal again. Therefore, even when \\(a\\) is a perfect square, they are equal.\n\nAlternatively, if \\(a\\) is a perfect fourth power, say \\(a = k^4\\), then:\n\nLeft side:\n1. \\(\\sqrt{k^4} = k^2\\)\n2. \\(\\lfloor k^2 \\rfloor = k^2\\)\n3. \\(\\sqrt{k^2} = k\\)\n4. \\(\\lfloor k \\rfloor = k\\)\n\nRight side:\n1. \\(\\sqrt{\\sqrt{k^4}} = \\sqrt{k^2} = k\\)\n2. \\(\\lfloor k \\rfloor = k\\)\n\nEqual. \n\nAnother angle: Suppose that the two functions \\(\\lfloor \\sqrt{\\lfloor \\sqrt{a} \\rfloor} \\rfloor\\) and \\(\\lfloor \\sqrt{\\sqrt{a}} \\rfloor\\) are actually identical functions for \\(a > 1\\). Given the analysis and examples, this seems plausible. Therefore, the equality is necessarily true.\n\nThus, after going through multiple examples and analyzing the intervals where the floor functions operate, it appears that the two expressions are indeed equal for all \\(a > 1\\). There doesn't seem to be any counterexample where they differ. Therefore, the answer should be that the equality is necessarily true.", "deepseek_solution": "The equality \\(\\left\\lfloor \\sqrt{\\left\\lfloor \\sqrt{a} \\right\\rfloor} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor \\sqrt{\\sqrt{a}} \\right\\rfloor\\) holds for all \\(a > 1\\). \n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Definitions and Setup:**\n   - Let \\(m = \\left\\lfloor \\sqrt{a} \\right\\rfloor\\). This means \\(m \\leq \\sqrt{a} < m + 1\\), so \\(m^2 \\leq a < (m + 1)^2\\).\n   - The left-hand side (LHS) becomes \\(\\left\\lfloor \\sqrt{m} \\right\\rfloor\\).\n   - The right-hand side (RHS) is \\(\\left\\lfloor a^{1/4} \\right\\rfloor\\), since \\(\\sqrt{\\sqrt{a}} = a^{1/4}\\).\n\n2. **Interval Analysis:**\n   - For \\(a \\in [k^4, (k + 1)^4)\\), the fourth root satisfies \\(k \\leq a^{1/4} < k + 1\\), so \\(\\left\\lfloor a^{1/4} \\right\\rfloor = k\\).\n 