s approximately -0.3, which is greater than -0.5. Therefore, the critical points within the domain are:\n\nWithin the domain x \u2208 (-0.3025, 3.3025), excluding 0 and 3. So, x = -0.5 is outside the domain (since -0.5 < -0.3025). Therefore, within the domain, 2x + 4 and 2x + 1 are:\n\nFor x \u2208 (-0.3025, 3.3025):\n\n2x + 4: Let's compute at x = -0.3025: 2*(-0.3025) + 4 \u2248 -0.605 + 4 \u2248 3.395 > 0. So, 2x + 4 is always positive in the domain.\n\n2x + 1: At x = -0.3025: 2*(-0.3025) + 1 \u2248 -0.605 + 1 \u2248 0.395 > 0. Similarly, for all x in the domain, since x \u2265 -0.3025, 2x \u2265 -0.605, so 2x +1 \u2265 -0.605 +1 = 0.395 >0. Hence, both 2x +4 and 2x +1 are positive in the entire domain. Therefore, the absolute values can be removed without changing sign:\n\n|2x +4| - |2x +1| = (2x +4) - (2x +1) = 3.\n\nSo, the numerator simplifies to 3.\n\nNow, the denominator is 3x + 4.5. Let's note that 4.5 is 9/2, so 3x + 9/2. Alternatively, 3x + 4.5 = 3(x + 1.5).\n\nThe square root term is sqrt(x\u00b2 + 3x + 2.25). Let me check the expression under the square root:\n\nx\u00b2 + 3x + 2.25. Let's see if this is a perfect square. Let's complete the square:\n\nx\u00b2 + 3x + (9/4) = (x + 1.5)^2. But 2.25 is 9/4? Wait, 2.25 is 9/4? No, 9/4 is 2.25. Yes, because 3/2 squared is 9/4. So x\u00b2 + 3x + 2.25 = (x + 1.5)^2. Therefore, sqrt(x\u00b2 + 3x + 2.25) = |x + 1.5|. But since x is in the domain (-0.3025, 3.3025), x + 1.5 will be in (-0.3025 +1.5, 3.3025 +1.5) \u2248 (1.1975, 4.8025). So x +1.5 is positive, hence sqrt(...) = x +1.5.\n\nTherefore, the RHS simplifies to [3 / (3x +4.5)] * (x +1.5).\n\nSimplify:\n\n3 / (3x + 4.5) = 3 / [3(x + 1.5)] = 1/(x +1.5). So multiplying by (x +1.5):\n\n[1/(x +1.5)] * (x +1.5) = 1.\n\nTherefore, the equation simplifies to:\n\ny -4 = 1 \u21d2 y = 5.\n\nSo, the coordinates of point M(x\u2080, y\u2080) lie on y = 5. But also, they must satisfy the original logarithm's validity. So, the argument of the log on the left side is y -4 =