e tangents to be perpendicular, the product of their slopes must be \\(-1\\). Using the parametric form of the parabola and the quadratic equation for the slopes of the tangents, we find that the product of the roots of the quadratic equation in \\(t\\) (related to the slopes) must satisfy:\n\n\\[\n4a(y_0 - 1) = -1\n\\]\n\nGiven \\(y_0 = 5\\), we solve for \\(a\\):\n\n\\[\n4a(5 - 1) = -1 \\implies 4a \\cdot 4 = -1 \\implies a = -\\frac{1}{16}\n\\]\n\nThus, the target function is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{y = -\\dfrac{1}{16}(x + 1)^2 + 1}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Transform the function:**\n   Start from the given function \\(y = a(x + 1)^{2} + 1\\).\n\n   \\[\n   y - 1 = a(x + 1)^{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Let \\(u = y - 1\\) and \\(z = x + 1\\), then the equation becomes:\n\n   \\[\n   u = az^{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Define points and tangents:**\n   \n   Consider points on the parabola \\(A(z_{1}, az_{1}^{2})\\) and \\(B(z_{2}, az_{2}^{2})\\), where the tangents at these points intersect at point \\((z_{0}, u_{0})\\).\n\n   The tangent at point \\(A\\) (using the derivative):\n\n   \\[\n   u - az_{1}^{2} = 2az_{1}(z - z_{1})\n   \\]\n\n   Since this line passes through \\((z_{0}, u_{0})\\):\n\n   \\[\n   u_{0} - az_{1}^{2} = 2az_{1}(z_{0} - z_{1})\n   \\]\n\n   Simplify to:\n\n   \\[\n   u_{0} = 2az_{1}z_{0} - az_{1}^{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Repeat for point \\(B\\):**\n\n   Using the tangent at point \\(B\\):\n\n   \\[\n   u - az_{2}^{2} = 2az_{2}(z - z_{2})\n   \\]\n\n   Since this line also passes through \\((z_{0}, u_{0})\\):\n\n   \\[\n   u_{0} - az_{2}^{2} = 2az_{2}(z_{0} - z_{2})\n   \\]\n\n   Simplify to:\n\n   \\[\n   u_{0} = 2az_{2}z_{0} - az_{2}^{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Relate the two equations:**\n   \n   Subtract the two relations:\n\n   \\[\n   2az_{1}z_{0} - az_{1}^{2} = 2az_{2}z_{0} - az_{2}^{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Simplify to:\n\n   \\[\n   2az_{1}z_{0} - 2az_{2}z_{0} = az_{1}^{2} - az_{2}^{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Factor out \\(a\\):\n\n   \\[\n   2a(z_{1} - z_{2})z_{0} = a(z_{1