ng at C (0,1), moving towards Z (z,0). Let's use parameter t \u2208 [0,1]:\n\nx = z*t\n\ny = 1 - t\n\nSo, coordinates on CZ are (z*t, 1 - t)\n\nSo, M must satisfy:\n\n( (1 - x)/2, x/2 ) = (z*t, 1 - t)\n\nTherefore, equations:\n\n(1 - x)/2 = z*t\n\nx/2 = 1 - t\n\nFrom the second equation: t = 1 - x/2\n\nSubstitute into the first equation:\n\n(1 - x)/2 = z*(1 - x/2)\n\nMultiply both sides by 2:\n\n1 - x = 2z*(1 - x/2)\n\nExpand the right side:\n\n2z - z x\n\nTherefore:\n\n1 - x = 2z - z x\n\nBring all terms to the left:\n\n1 - x - 2z + z x = 0\n\nFactor:\n\n1 - 2z - x(1 - z) = 0\n\nHmm. Let's plug in z = 3 - 1/x from earlier.\n\nSo, z = 3 - 1/x.\n\nTherefore:\n\n1 - 2*(3 - 1/x) - x*(1 - (3 - 1/x)) = 0\n\nSimplify term by term:\n\n1 - 6 + 2/x - x*(1 - 3 + 1/x) = 0\n\nSimplify:\n\n-5 + 2/x - x*(-2 + 1/x) = 0\n\nMultiply out the last term:\n\n-5 + 2/x + 2x - x*(1/x) = 0\n\nSimplify x*(1/x) = 1:\n\n-5 + 2/x + 2x - 1 = 0\n\nCombine constants:\n\n-6 + 2/x + 2x = 0\n\nMultiply both sides by x to eliminate the denominator:\n\n-6x + 2 + 2x^2 = 0\n\nRearrange:\n\n2x^2 - 6x + 2 = 0\n\nDivide by 2:\n\nx^2 - 3x + 1 = 0\n\nSolve this quadratic equation:\n\nx = [3 \u00b1 sqrt(9 - 4)]/2 = [3 \u00b1 sqrt(5)]/2\n\nBut we know that x must be between 1/3 and 1/2 (approx 0.333 to 0.5). Let's compute the roots:\n\nsqrt(5) \u2248 2.236, so:\n\nx = [3 + 2.236]/2 \u2248 5.236/2 \u2248 2.618, which is greater than 1, invalid.\n\nx = [3 - 2.236]/2 \u2248 0.764/2 \u2248 0.382, which is approximately 0.382, which is between 1/3 (\u22480.333) and 1/2 (0.5). So x \u2248 0.381966.\n\nSo x = (3 - sqrt(5))/2 \u2248 (3 - 2.236)/2 \u2248 0.764/2 \u2248 0.382, which is indeed between 1/3 and 1/2. So x = (3 - sqrt(5))/2.\n\nTherefore, x = (3 - sqrt(5))/2.\n\nThen, from earlier, z = 3 - 1/x. Let's compute that.\n\nx = (3 - sqrt(5))/2\n\nSo 1/x = 2/(3 - sqrt(5)) = multiply numerator and denominator by (3 + sqrt(5)):\n\n2*(3 + sqrt(5))/[(3 - sqrt(5))(3 + sqrt(5))] = 2*(3 + sqrt(5))/(9 - 5) = 2*(3 + sqrt(5))/