 + 2\\sqrt{2}}{6}}, \\quad \\sin \\left( \\frac{B}{2} \\right) = \\sqrt{\\frac{1 - \\cos B}{2}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{3 - 2\\sqrt{2}}{6}}\n     \\]\n   - For \\( \\sin \\left( \\frac{A}{2} \\right) \\) and \\( \\cos \\left( \\frac{A}{2} \\right) \\):\n     \\[\n     \\sin \\left( \\frac{A}{2} \\right) = \\sqrt{\\frac{1 - \\cos A}{2}}, \\quad \\cos \\left( \\frac{A}{2} \\right) = \\sqrt{\\frac{1 + \\cos A}{2}}\n     \\]\n   - Regardless of \\( A \\) being acute or obtuse, the product simplifies to:\n     \\[\n     \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\right) \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{3} \\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt{90}}{9} = \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}\n     \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( \\sin \\frac{A-B}{2} + \\sin \\frac{C}{2} \\) is \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo find \\(\\sin \\frac{A-B}{2} + \\sin \\frac{C}{2}\\) in \\(\\triangle ABC\\) given \\(a = 2b\\) and \\(\\cos B = \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\), we proceed as follows:\n\n1. **Introduction of Variables**:\n   \n   Define:\n   \\[\n   \\sin \\frac{A}{2} = m, \\quad \\cos \\frac{A}{2} = n, \\quad \\sin \\frac{B}{2} = x, \\quad \\cos \\frac{B}{2} = y \\quad (m, n, x, y > 0)\n   \\]\n\n2. **Finding \\(y\\) and \\(x\\)**:\n   \n   Use the identity for \\(\\cos B\\):\n   \\[\n   \\cos B = 2y^2 - 1\n   \\]\n   Given \\(\\cos B = \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\):\n   \\[\n   2y^2 - 1 = \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\implies 2y^2 = 1 + \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\implies 2y^2 = \\frac{3 + 2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\n   \\]\n   Thus:\n   \\[\n   y^2 = \\frac{2\\sqrt{2} + 3}{6} \\implies y = \\sqrt{\\frac{2\\sqrt{2} + 3}{6}}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying \\(y\\):\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{\\sqrt{2} + 1}{\\sqrt{6}}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Finding \\(x\\)**:\n   \n   Similarly, for \\(\\sin \\frac{B}{2} = x\\), since \\(\\sin^2 \\frac{B}{2} + \\cos^2 \\frac{B}{2} = 1:\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x^2 + y^2 = 1 \\implies x^2 + \\frac{2\\sqrt{2} + 3}{6} = 1 \\implies x^2 = 1 - \\frac{2\\sqrt{2} + 3}{6} = \\frac{3 - 2\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\implies x = \\frac{\\sqrt{2} - 