od3, y=-2\u22611 mod3 (since -2\u22611 mod3). Fits the first case.\n\nSo these solutions fit the modulo3 conditions. If there are other solutions, they must also fit these cases.\n\nLet me check if there are other solutions where x\u22610 mod3 and y\u22611 mod3 or x\u22612 mod3 and y\u22610 mod3.\n\nLet me consider x\u22612 mod3 and y\u22610 mod3. So y=3k. Let y=3k. Then x=3m +2. Substitute into equation:\n\n(3m +2)^3 - (3k)^3 =2*(3m +2)*(3k) +8.\n\nExpand left side:\n\n27m\u00b3 +54m\u00b2 +36m +8 -27k\u00b3.\n\nRight side:\n\n2*(9mk +6k) +8 =18mk +12k +8.\n\nSet equal:\n\n27m\u00b3 +54m\u00b2 +36m +8 -27k\u00b3 =18mk +12k +8.\n\nSubtract 8 from both sides:\n\n27m\u00b3 +54m\u00b2 +36m -27k\u00b3 =18mk +12k.\n\nDivide both sides by 3:\n\n9m\u00b3 +18m\u00b2 +12m -9k\u00b3 =6mk +4k.\n\nRearranged:\n\n9m\u00b3 +18m\u00b2 +12m -6mk -4k -9k\u00b3 =0.\n\nThis seems complicated. Maybe try small m and k.\n\nLet\u2019s start with k=0: y=0. Then equation becomes x\u00b3=8\u2192x=2. Which is our solution (2,0). So k=0 gives known solution.\n\nk=1: y=3. Then x=3m +2. Substitute into original equation:\n\n(3m +2)^3 -27 =2*(3m +2)*3 +8.\n\nCompute left:27m\u00b3 +54m\u00b2 +36m +8 -27=27m\u00b3 +54m\u00b2 +36m -19.\n\nRight: 18m +12 +8=18m +20.\n\nSet equal:27m\u00b3 +54m\u00b2 +36m -19=18m +20.\n\nBring all terms left:\n\n27m\u00b3 +54m\u00b2 +18m -39=0.\n\nDivide by 3:\n\n9m\u00b3 +18m\u00b2 +6m -13=0.\n\nTry m=0: -13\u22600. m=1:9 +18 +6 -13=20\u22600. m=-1:-9 +18 -6 -13=-10\u22600. No solutions.\n\nk=2: y=6. x=3m +2.\n\nEquation:\n\n(3m +2)^3 -216=2*(3m +2)*6 +8.\n\nLeft:27m\u00b3 +54m\u00b2 +36m +8 -216=27m\u00b3 +54m\u00b2 +36m -208.\n\nRight: 36m +24 +8=36m +32.\n\nSet equal:\n\n27m\u00b3 +54m\u00b2 +36m -208=36m +32.\n\nBring left:\n\n27m\u00b3 +54m\u00b2 -0m -240=0.\n\nDivide by 3:\n\n9m\u00b3 +18m\u00b2 -80=0.\n\nTry m=2:9*8 +18*4 -80=72 +72 -80=64\u22600. m=1:9 +18 -80=-53\u22600. m=3:243 +162 -80=325\u22600. No solution.\n\nSimilarly, larger k makes left side even larger, so probably no solutions here.\n\nNow check other case: x\u22610 mod3 and y\u22611 mod3. So x=3m, y=3k +1. Substitute into equation:\n\n(3m)^3 - (3k +1)^3 =2*(3m)*(3k +1) +8.\n\nCompute left side:\n\n27m\u00b3 - (27k\u00b3 +27k\u00b2 +9k +1)=27m\u00b3 -27k\u00b3 -27k\u00b2 -9k -1.\n\nRight side:\n\n18m*(3k +1) +8=54mk +18m +8.\n\nSet equal:\n\n27m\u00b3 -27k\u00b3 -27k\u00b2 -9k -1=54mk +18m +8.\n\nBring all terms left:\n\n27m\u00b3 -27k\u00b3 -27k\u00b2 -9k -1 -54mk -18m -8=0.\n\nSimplify:\n\n27m\u00b3 -27k\u00b3 -27k\u00b2 -54mk -18m -9k -9=0.\n\nDivide by 9:\n\n3m\u00b3 -3k\u00b3 -3k\u00b2 -6mk -2m -k -1=0.\n\nThis seems complex. Let me try small m and k.\n\nStart with k=0: y=1. Then equation:\n\n3m\u00b3 -0 -0 -0 -2m -0 -1=0\u21923m\u00b3 -2m -1=0.\n\nTry m=1:3 -2 -1=0\u21920=0. So m=1. So x=3*1=3, y=1.\n\nCheck solution (3,1):\n\nLeft:3\u00b3 -1\u00b3=27 -1=26.\n\nRight:2*3*1 +8=6 +8=14. 26\u226014. Not a solution. Wait, this contradicts. Wait, when k=0, y=3*0 +1=1, x=3m. For m=1, equation gives 3*1 -2*1 -1=0. But actual substitution into original equation gives 27 -1=26 vs 14. So something's wrong. Did I make a mistake in substitution?\n\nWait, let's verify:\n\nOriginal equation with x=3m=3*1=3, y=3k +1=1:\n\n3\u00b3 -1\u00b3=27 -1=26.\n\n2*3*1 +8=6 +8=14. 26\u226014. So no solution here. So even though m=1 satisfies the reduced equation 3m\u00b3 -2m -1=0, it doesn't satisfy the original. This is because when we divided by 9, we might have introduced solutions that don't satisfy the original equation. Hmm.\n\nWait, the division by 9 is valid because we subtracted and moved terms, but the original equation would have to hold. Wait, maybe m=1, k=0 gives x=3, y=1, but in reality, the original equation isn't satisfied. Therefore, perhaps there was an error in the algebra.\n\nWait, let's redo the substitution for x=3m, y=3k +1.\n\nOriginal equation:\n\n(3m)^3 - (3k +1)^3 = 2*(3m)*(3k +1) +8.\n\nLeft side:27m\u00b3 - (27k\u00b3 +27k\u00b2 +9k +1)=27m\u00b3 -27k\u00b3 -27k\u00b2 -9k -1.\n\nRight side: 2*3m*(3k +1) +8=6m*(3k +1) +8=18mk +6m +8.\n\nSet equal:\n\n27m\u00b3 -27k\u00b3 -27k\u00b2 -9k -1 =18mk +6m +8.\n\nBring all terms left:\n\n27m\u00b3 -27k\u00b3 -27k\u00b2 -9k -1 -18mk -6m -8=0.\n\nSimplify:\n\n27m\u00b3 -27k\u00b3 -27k\u00b2 -18mk -6m -9k -9=0.\n\nDivide by 9:\n\n3m\u00b3 -3k\u00b3 -3k\u00b2 -2mk - (2/3)m -k -1=0.\n\nWait, here's a mistake. When dividing by 9, the term -6m becomes - (6/9)m = - (2/3)m, which is not integer. This suggests that dividing by 9 was not appropriate because not all coefficients are divisible by 9. Indeed, looking back:\n\nOriginal equation after moving terms:\n\n27m\u00b3 -27k\u00b3 -27k\u00b2 -18mk -6m -9k -9=0.\n\nCheck each term:\n\n27m\u00b3 divisible by9: yes, 3*9=27.\n\n-27k\u00b3: divisible by9.\n\n-27k\u00b2: divisible by9.\n\n-18mk: divisible by9? 18=2*9, so yes.\n\n-6m: 6 is not divisible by9, so term -6m/9= -2m/3.\n\nSimilarly -9k divisible by9.\n\n-9 divisible by9.\n\nSo dividing by9 gives:\n\n3m\u00b3 -3k\u00b3 -3k\u00b2 -2mk - (2m/3) -k -1=0.\n\nBut m and k are integers, so -2m/3 must be integer. Therefore, m must be divisible by3. Let m=3n. Then substitute m=3n:\n\n3*(27n\u00b3) -3k\u00b3 -3k\u00b2 -2*3n*k -2*(3n)/3 -k -1=0.\n\nWait, this is getting too convoluted. Maybe instead of dividing by9, look for integer solutions where 27m\u00b3 -27k\u00b3 -27k\u00b2 -18mk -6m -9k -9=0.\n\nThis seems very difficult. Let's try small k and see if any m makes sense.\n\nk=0:\n\n27m\u00b3 -0 -0 -0 -6m -0 -9=0\u219227m\u00b3 -6m -9=0.\n\nDivide by3:9m\u00b3 -2m -3=0.\n\nTry m=1:9 -2 -3=4\u22600. m=0:0 -0 -3=-3\u22600. m=-1:-9 +2 -3=-10\u22600. No solution.\n\nk=1:\n\n27m\u00b3 -27 -27 -18m -6m -9 -9=27m\u00b3 -27 -27 -24m -18=27m\u00b3 -24m -72=0.\n\nDivide by3:9m\u00b3 -8m -24=0.\n\nTry m=2:9*8 -16 -24=72 -16 -24=32\u22600. m=1:9 -8 -24=-23\u22600. m=3:9*27 -24 -24=243 -48=195\u22600. No.\n\nk=2:\n\n27m\u00b3 -27*8 -27*4 -18m*2 -6m -9*2 -9=27m\u00b3 -216 -108 -36m -6m -18 -9=27