g the equation modulo 2 and modulo 3:\n     - Modulo 2 analysis showed \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) must have the same parity.\n     - Modulo 3 analysis showed possible solutions must satisfy \\(x \\equiv 0 \\pmod{3}\\) and \\(y \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3}\\) or \\(x \\equiv 2 \\pmod{3}\\) and \\(y \\equiv 0 \\pmod{3}\\).\n\n4. **Verification of Solutions:**\n   - The solutions \\((2, 0)\\) and \\((0, -2)\\) were verified to satisfy the original equation.\n   - Further analysis and testing of other potential values and cases confirmed no additional solutions.\n\nThus, the integer solutions to the equation \\(x^3 - y^3 = 2xy + 8\\) are:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(2, 0)} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\boxed{(0, -2)}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Case \\(x = 0\\):**\n   - Substitute \\(x = 0\\) into the equation \\(x^3 - y^3 = 2xy + 8\\):\n     \\[\n     0^3 - y^3 = 2 \\cdot 0 \\cdot y + 8 \\implies -y^3 = 8 \\implies y^3 = -8 \\implies y = -2\n     \\]\n   - Therefore, one solution is \\((0, -2)\\).\n\n2. **Case \\(y = 0\\):**\n   - Substitute \\(y = 0\\) into the equation \\(x^3 - y^3 = 2xy + 8\\):\n     \\[\n     x^3 - 0^3 = 2x \\cdot 0 + 8 \\implies x^3 = 8 \\implies x = 2\n     \\]\n   - Therefore, another solution is \\((2, 0)\\).\n\n3. **Case \\(x > 0\\) and \\(y < 0\\):**\n   - If \\(x > 0\\) and \\(y < 0\\), the equation \\(x^3 = y^3 + 2xy + 8\\) leads to:\n     \\[\n     x^3 < 8 \\implies x = 1^3 \\leq \\left| y^3 \\right|\n     \\]\n   - Given \\(y\\) is a negative integer:\n     \\[\n     1^3 = y^3 + 2 \\cdot 1 \\cdot y + 8 \\implies y^3 + 2y + 7 = 0\n     \\]\n   - The cubic equation \\(y^3 + 2y + 7 = 0\\) has no integer solutions. Therefore, this case is impossible.\n\n4. **Case \\(x < 0\\) and \\(y > 0\\):**\n   - If \\(x < 0\\) and \\(y > 0\\), analyze \\(y^3 - x^3 = -2xy - 8\\):\n     \\[\n     y^3 - x^3 = -2xy - 8 < -2xy \\quad (\\text{as } x \\text{ is negative})\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     y^3 + (-x)^3 > -2xy \\quad \\text{(check positivity of ) } (x^3 > y^3)\n     \\]\n   - This creates a contradiction due to conflicting inequalities. Therefore, this case is impossible.\n\n5. **Case \\(xy > 0\\):**\n   - If \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) have same sign:\n     \\[\n     x^3 - y^3 = 2xy + 8 > 0\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     x^3 - y^3 = (x-y)(x^2 + xy + y^2) > 0 \\implies x > y\n     \\]\n\n   - **Sub-case \\(x - y = 1\\):**\n     \\[\n     2xy + 8 = (x - y)(x^2 + xy + y^2)\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     2xy + 8 = 1 + 3xy \\quad (\\because x = y + 1)\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     2(y+1)(y) + 8 = 1 + 3(y + 1) y\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     2(y^2 + y) + 8 = 1 + 3(y^2 + y)\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     2y^2 + 2y + 8 = 1 + 3y^2 + 3y\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\Rightarrow y^2 + y - 7 = 0\n     \\]\n     - The quadratic equation \\( y^2 + y - 7 = 0\\) has no integer solutions.\n\n   - **Sub-case \\(x - y \\geq 2\\):**\n     \\[\n     x^3 - y^3 = (x-y)(x^2 + xy + y^2) \\geq 2(4 + 3xy) = 8 + 6xy\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     2xy + 8 \\geq 8 + 6xy \\implies -4xy \\leq 0\n     \\]\n     - The derived inequality conflicts with \\(xy > 0\\) assumption and hence is impossible.\n\n### Conclusion\nThe only two possible solutions for integer pairs \\((x, y)\\) are:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(0, -2) \\text{ and } (2, 0)}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Someone thought of \\( n \\) non-negative integers (not necessarily distinct) and wrote down all \\( (2^n - 1) \\) possible sums that can be formed from them on a sheet of paper. Can the original numbers be determined from these sums?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so the problem is: If someone thinks of n non-negative integers and writes down all (2^n - 1) possible sums formed from these numbers, can we determine the original numbers from these sums? Hmm, interesting. Let me try to break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, let's understand what exactly is being asked. We have n numbers, say a1, a2, ..., an. Each of these numbers is a non-negative integer. Then, all possible no