ely 0.8616 or 0.2461.\n\nBut previously, we found cos C = 16/65 \u2248 0.24615. So that matches the second solution. So why is there another solution?\n\nBut cosine of an angle in a triangle must be between -1 and 1, and since angle C is between 0 and \u03c0, cos C can be between -1 and 1. Both solutions are within that range. But which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe we need to check which one is consistent with the triangle.\n\nWait, angle C is equal to \u03c0 - A - B. If angle A is acute (which we determined earlier), and angle B is acute, then angle C is \u03c0 - (acute + acute) which could be either acute or obtuse depending on the sum. Since angles A and B sum to less than \u03c0, angle C is positive. But could angle C be obtuse?\n\nIf angles A and B sum to less than \u03c0/2, then angle C would be greater than \u03c0/2. Let's check.\n\nWe know angle A: sin A = 3/5, so angle A \u2248 arcsin(3/5) \u2248 36.87 degrees.\n\nAngle B: cos B = 5/13, so angle B \u2248 arccos(5/13) \u2248 67.38 degrees.\n\nSum of angles A and B \u2248 36.87 + 67.38 \u2248 104.25 degrees. Therefore, angle C \u2248 180 - 104.25 \u2248 75.75 degrees, which is acute. Therefore, cos C should be positive and approximately cos 75.75 degrees \u2248 0.246, which is 16/65 \u2248 0.24615. That matches the second solution. The first solution, 0.8616, would correspond to an angle of about 30.4 degrees, but that would mean angles A and B sum to 149.6 degrees, which contradicts our previous calculation. Therefore, the first solution is invalid. \n\nTherefore, the correct value of cos C is 16/65. This confirms the earlier result.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another approach with coordinates.\n\nSuppose we place triangle ABC in coordinate system. Let\u2019s place angle A at the origin, side AB along the x-axis. Let\u2019s let AB = c, BC = a, AC = b.\n\nBut maybe that's complicate. Alternatively, construct right triangles based on the given sine and cosine.\n\nFor angle A, sin A = 3/5. If we consider a right triangle with angle A, opposite side 3, hypotenuse 5, then adjacent side is 4 (since 3-4-5 triangle). So cos A = 4/5.\n\nSimilarly, for angle B, cos B = 5/13. So in a right triangle, adjacent side 5, hypotenuse 13, so opposite side is 12. Therefore, sin B = 12/13.\n\nNow, in triangle ABC, angles A, B, C. Let's assign lengths using the Law of Sines.\n\nLet\u2019s let the sides opposite angles A, B, C be a, b, c respectively.\n\nFrom Law of Sines:\n\na / sin A = b / sin B = c / sin C = 2R.\n\nLet\u2019s assume 2R = 65 (just a multiple to make the denominators go away). So:\n\na = 2R sin A = 65*(3/5) = 39\n\nb = 2R sin B = 65*(12/13) = 60\n\nc = 2R sin C = 65 sin C\n\nNow, using the Law of Cosines on angle C:\n\nc\u00b2 = a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 - 2ab cos C\n\nSubstitute c = 65 sin C, a = 39, b = 60:\n\n(65 sin C)\u00b2 = 39\u00b2 + 60\u00b2 - 2*39*60 cos C\n\nCompute left side: 4225 sin\u00b2 C\n\nRight side: 1521 + 3600 - 4680 cos C = 5121 - 4680 cos C\n\nSo equation:\n\n4225 sin\u00b2 C = 5121 - 4680 cos C\n\nAgain, using sin\u00b2 C = 1 - cos\u00b2 C:\n\n4225(1 - cos\u00b2 C) = 5121 - 4680 cos C\n\nExpand left side:\n\n4225 - 4225 cos\u00b2 C = 5121 - 4680 cos C\n\nBring all terms to left side:\n\n4225 - 4225 cos\u00b2 C - 5121 + 4680 cos C = 0\n\nSimplify constants:\n\n4225 - 5121 = -896\n\nSo:\n\n-4225 cos\u00b2 C + 4680 cos C - 896 = 0\n\nMultiply both sides by -1:\n\n4225 cos\u00b2 C - 4680 cos C + 896 = 0\n\nDivide all terms by GCD of coefficients. Let's see, 4225, 4680, 896.\n\nCheck GCD(4225, 4680). 4225 factors: 5^2 * 13 * 13? Wait, 4225 divided by 25 is 169, which is 13\u00b2. So 4225 = 25*169 = 5\u00b2*13\u00b2.\n\n4680: 4680 / 10 = 468. 468 / 12 = 39. 39 = 13*3. So 4680 = 10*12*39 = 10*12*13*3. So prime factors: 2^3 * 3^2 * 5 * 13.\n\n896: 896 / 2 = 448; /2=224; /2=112; /2=56; /2=28; /2=14; /2=7. So 2^7 *7.\n\nSo common factors: 4225 and 4680 share 5 and 13. 896 shares no common factors. So GCD is 5*13=65? Let's check:\n\n4225 / 65 = 65, 4680 / 65 =