, q)\\) inside the triangle.\n\n2. **Lines Parallel to Sides**:\n   - A line through \\(O\\) parallel to \\(BC\\) intersects \\(AB\\) at \\((p + q, 0)\\) and \\(AC\\) at \\((0, p + q)\\).\n   - A line through \\(O\\) parallel to \\(AB\\) intersects \\(BC\\) at \\((1 - q, q)\\) and \\(AC\\) at \\((0, q)\\).\n   - A line through \\(O\\) parallel to \\(AC\\) intersects \\(AB\\) at \\((p, 0)\\) and \\(BC\\) at \\((p, 1 - p)\\).\n\n3. **Cevians \\(AA_1\\), \\(BB_1\\), \\(CC_1\\)**:\n   - \\(AA_1\\) connects \\(A\\) to \\((1 - q, q)\\) on \\(BC\\).\n   - \\(BB_1\\) connects \\(B\\) to \\((0, p + q)\\) on \\(AC\\).\n   - \\(CC_1\\) connects \\(C\\) to \\((p, 0)\\) on \\(AB\\).\n\n4. **Area Calculations**:\n   - The areas of the triangles adjacent to \\(A\\), \\(B\\), and \\(C\\) are calculated using coordinate geometry and determinant formulas.\n   - The central triangle's area is derived from the intersection of the lines parallel to the sides through \\(O\\).\n\n5. **Key Insight**:\n   - The triangles adjacent to the vertices and the central triangle are similar to the original triangle \\(ABC\\).\n   - The sum of the areas of the triangles adjacent to the vertices is shown to be equal to the area of the central triangle using properties of similar triangles and area ratios.\n\n### Final Answer\nThe sum of the areas of the triangles adjacent to vertices \\(A\\), \\(B\\), and \\(C\\) is equal to the area of the fourth triangle. Therefore, the result is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The sum of the areas of the triangles adjacent to } A, B, \\text{ and } C \\text{ equals the area of the fourth triangle.}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\nWe are given that point \\( O \\) lies inside triangle \\( ABC \\), and segments \\( AA_1 \\), \\( BB_1 \\), and \\( CC_1 \\) are drawn parallel to the sides of the triangle, dividing it into four triangles and three quadrilaterals. We aim to show that the sum of the areas of the triangles that share a vertex with \\( A \\), \\( B \\), and \\( 