swer should be 11 rounds.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe minimum number of rounds required is \\boxed{11}.", "deepseek_solution": "To determine the minimum number of rounds required to discard all stones from 1990 piles, where each pile has a number of stones from 1 to 1990, we need to consider the binary representation of the number of stones in each pile. \n\nIn each round, we can remove the same number of stones from any number of piles. The key insight is that each number can be represented in binary, and each bit position corresponds to a power of two. For each bit position, we can remove the corresponding power of two from all piles that have that bit set. This approach ensures that we efficiently remove stones in the minimum number of rounds.\n\nThe largest pile has 1990 stones. Converting 1990 to binary gives \\(11111000110_2\\), which has 11 bits. Therefore, we need to process each bit position from the least significant bit to the most significant bit, requiring one round per bit position. \n\nThus, the minimum number of rounds required is the number of bits in the binary representation of 1990, which is 11.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{11}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Understanding the problem:**\n   - We start with 1990 piles of stones, where each pile contains a different number of stones ranging from 1 to 1990.\n   - In each round, we are allowed to remove a certain number of stones from any subset of these piles as long as we remove the same number of stones from each chosen pile.\n   - The goal is to determine the minimum number of rounds required to remove all the stones from all the piles.\n\n2. **Grouping strategy:**\n   - After each round, we can group the piles based on the remaining number of stones. \n   - We also consider empty piles as a separate group.\n   - At some moment, assume we have \\( n \\) different groups of piles (each group containing piles with the same number of stones).\n   - In the next round, if we remove stones from piles that belong to \\( k \\) different groups, these piles will still belong to \\( k \\) groups (because the number of stones removed is the same from each pile).\n   - The remaining piles that were not chosen to remove stones in the current round will belong to \\( n - k \\) groups.\n\n3. **Maximum reduction analysis:**\n   - The number of groups \\( n \\) can reduce at most to \\( \\max \\{k, n-k\\} \\) after each round.\n   - This is because the chosen \\( k \\) groups still remain distinct after removal and the \\( n-k \\) groups remain unchanged.\n\n4. **Reduction to half:**\n   - This implies that the number of groups \\( n \\) can at most reduce approximately to half after each round.\n   - Thus, the number of rounds required to reduce the number of groups from 1990 to 1 can be estimated by repeatedly halving 1990:\n     \\[\n     1990, 995, 498, 249, 125, 63, 32, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1\n     \\]\n   - Each step of this sequence represents one round. Counting the steps gives us 11 rounds.\n\n5. **Validation with examples:**\n   - We start with piles: \\( 1, 2, \\ldots, 1990 \\).\n   - Round 1: Remove 995 stones from each pile with at least 995 stones:\n     \\[\n     995, 994, \\ldots, 1,0\n     \\]\n     (each pile now has at most 995 stones)\n   - Round 2: Remove 498 stones from each pile with at least 498 stones:\n     \\[\n     497, 496, \\ldots, 1, 0\n     \\]\n     (each pile now has at most 497 stones)\n   - Continue similarly for the next rounds, removing amounts corresponding to the sequence above.\n\n6. **Conclusion:**\n   - Through this strategy, 11 rounds are indeed sufficient to remove all the stones from all piles.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{11}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Given a convex polygon \\( A_{n+1} \\) with \\( n+1 \\) sides (where \\( n \\geq 2 \\)), partition the polygon into \\( n-1 \\) triangles using \\( n-2 \\) non-intersecting diagonals (referred to as a triangulation of \\( A_{n+1}